<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7354"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Suspiria_2<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Suspiria_2<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 14, 2005 - 2:15 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->what about basements?  attics? <BR> <BR>this &#34;watching without being seen&#34; thing bothers me, and perhaps he was quite skilled as a cat burglar and surveilled them from within their own homes.  it seems that a basement location pops up in many of the news stories, and seems to be a favorite location for him. <BR> <BR>any info on that would  be appreciated.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7354-->
<!--Post: 7382--><!--Time: 1108441965-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7382"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bestbud<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bestbud<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, February 14, 2005 - 8:32 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Most homes in Kansas have a basement...<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7382-->
<!--Post: 7408--><!--Time: 1108477276-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7408"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:whoisbtk@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Whoisbtk<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Whoisbtk<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 15, 2005 - 6:21 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I accessed Sedgwick County real-estate records.   The Bright house has been destroyed, and I can find no records for it.  I drive past it frequently, and it doesn&#39;t look like a basement was filled in as part of the demolition.  I&#39;ll stop and physically check the lot next time I&#39;m out that way. <BR> <BR>Otherwise: <BR> <BR>     Otero: full basement. <BR>     Fox: concrete crawl space. <BR>     Vian: crawl space. <BR>     Wegerly: part crawl space, part basement. <BR> <BR>[Note: a crawl space is typically about two feet high and extends under the home as opposed to the porch and garage, which typically are on a slab.  Water lines, sewer pipes, and heating ducts are usually what runs through these spaces.  The outside of the crawl space is made up of the foundation wall of the home; &#34;concrete crawl&#34; means the foundation is concrete.  They are vented to allow air circulation.  Access is gained by removing a cover plate and crawling in.] <BR> <BR>Never say never, but crawl spaces are &#42;never&#42; entered from inside the home.  There is no way to get into the house from this space. <BR> <BR>However it&#39;s quite possible for someone to crawl inside and wait for the occupant to return.  One could hear how many people were there from the crawl space.  These are unlit, dirty, nasty places where you literally crawl around.  No one checks these spaces for intruders the way they might check a basement. <BR> <BR>Basements are typically entered from the home.  There are two exceptions.  First are the windows.  In older homes there are &#34;casement windows&#34; set between the bottom of the house and ground level.  They are typically 18in. by 30 in. wide and narrow.  They hinge at the bottom and have a hand latch at the top.  Break one from the outside; reach in and turn the latch, push in and down, and someone can crawl into the basement, gaining access to the home. <BR> <BR>The other exception is a storm cellar door.  These are big &#40;like 4x8 feet&#41;, split down the middle, and hinged on the sides.  They slope away from the house at a shallow angle &#40;30 to 40 degrees.&#41;  Pull up and out, laying the door on the ground, and underneath are steps down to a full door, which opens into the basement.  The Wegerly home may have something like this, but none of the others do. <BR> <BR>As I recall, the only home that showed forced entry into the basement was Anna on Pinecrest.  But crawl spaces are built to be accessed from outside and would not show signs of forced entry. <BR> <BR>Depending on the location of the access panel or the vents, BTK could hide in a neighbor’s crawl space to watch the target home without being seen.  <BR> <BR>These would be perfect hiding/observation places. <BR> <BR><a href="mailto:whoisbtk@yahoo.com">whoisbtk@yahoo.com</a>  <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7408-->
<!--Post: 7520--><!--Time: 1108548836-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7520"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Suspiria_2<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Suspiria_2<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, February 16, 2005 - 2:13 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->thank you, exceedingly Whoisbtk! <BR> <BR>the detailed analysis is helpful for those not familiar with such spaces, but i was mainly wondering what is most typical in that part of the country.  it seems from your and Bestbud&#39;s posts that some kind of space under the house is rather typical, as it is also where i live. <BR> <BR>here we had a recent &#40;within the past 6 months&#41; break-in of our apartment building in which the locked storage spaces in the basement were all pried open with a crowbar and pilfered.  this is not a large building, and excluding the basement it is only 2 stories high, yet none of the residents was aware of the activities of the thieves until one went down to do laundry in the basement the next day. <BR> <BR>not that any wanted to know my life story, but it seems remarkeable what persons with nefarious intentions can get away with inside an occupied property in terms of noise.  since btk was probably just listening and/or watching, his activities would have been all the quieter.    <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7520-->
<!--Post: 7537--><!--Time: 1108564527-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7537"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Embersage<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Embersage<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, February 16, 2005 - 6:35 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I added a new thread under Vickie called linking the victims together, I have ask for help in  just the basics of the things the victims might have done together, After reading all the posts of each Victim I realized you all have great information, so if we narrow it down to the basics Like for example, some of the victims went to the same church, some of them took self defense classes, some worked in the same company, I believe we will see a pattern. I have ask for help in looking into the unsolved murders on the first page to see if any of those Victims have the same MO the 3 stab wounds, The 3 in most of their addresses, the strangulation with the cord, If we can link any of those murders to BTK we may get a more detailed Idea of where he is, Thanks for letting me help.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7537-->
<!--Post: 7860--><!--Time: 1108715561-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7860"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 18, 2005 - 12:32 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The victims all have one thing in common, BTK. And since, in his letters, he mentioned that he stalked them, it seems reasonable to assume that he didn&#39;t go door to door looking for victims.  <BR> <BR>What is making it hard to find a common denominator may be looking from the victims point of view. <BR> <BR>Instead of thinking &#34;what were they doing when BTK noticed them&#34;, try thinking &#34;I&#39;m BTK, where do I look for women to stalk?&#34;   <BR> <BR>EMBERSAGE: Thank you for the email, I still looking through it...your apostrophe key is putting up an &#39;edit/foriegn character&#39; when you use/used it. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7860-->
